{{Basis}}
The '''culture of Qym''' or the '''Qymish culture''' refers to both the national culture of the [[Qymish people]] and regional cultures within [[Qym]]. It has mainly been influenced by the [[Byrakans|Byrakan]], [[Ottelians|Ottelian]] and [[Sukats|Sukat]] cultures over the [[history of Qym]]. 

==Identity==
{{Main|Qymish people}}
{{See|Qymish nationalism}}

[[Qymish people]] are the main ethnic group and national identity of the inhabitants of [[Qym]]. Throughout Qymish history, many regional ethnic groups became Qymish through assimilation, while other groups retained their distinct ethnic identities, such as the [[Rumykian people|Rumykians]], the [[Emadinian people|Emadinians]], and the [[Sunamese people|Sunamese]]. Foreign ethnic groups have also migrated to Qym throughout history and in contemporary times, affecting the ethnic composition of the population. Based on modern-day DNA testing, Qymish people are mainly descendants of the [[Byrakans]]. 

===Role of the King===
{{Main|Follow the King}}

The [[King of Qym]] has an integral role in shaping and defining the Qymish national identity. The national motto, [[Follow the King]], reflects that role. U/C

Sociologist [[DUDE]] described the Qymish monarchy as a religion. U/C

==Languages==
{{Main|Qymish language}}

[[Qymish language|Qymish]], a [[Sukatic languages|Sukatic language]], is the sole official language of [[Qym]]. Most Qymish vocabulary is derived from the [[Sukat language]]. Significant minorities of words are derived from [[Ottelian language|Ottelian]] and [[Byrakan language|Byrakan]], with a smaller amount from [[OTHER ANCIENT MERANIAN CIV LANGUAGE]] and [[NEIGHBORING LANGUAGE]]. Qymish is written using the [[Sukatic script]]. 

The creation of the [[Royal academy of the Qymish language]] and many other academies in the {{abbr|21st|1661.5 to 1744.5 (Earth Years Equivalent)}} and {{abbr|22nd|1744.5 to 1828 (Earth Years Equivalent)}} centuries reinforced the unifying role of the Qymish language in culture, often at the expense of regional cultures. Regional languages native to Qym include [[Emadinian language|Emadinian]], [[Litagẽyan language|Litagẽyan]], [[Qarhõnian language|Qarhõnian]], [[Rumykian language|Rumykian]] and [[Sunamese language|Sunamese]]. Those five languages are recognized by the [[Regional Languages Royal Decree of 2353]], which guarantees access to compulsory education for speakers of those languages, as well as official status in administrative divisions where more than 25% of the population speak that language. 

The [[Rumykian people|Rumykians]], the [[Emadinian people|Emadinians]], the [[Sunamese people|Sunamese]], [[Litagẽyan people|Litagẽyans]], [[Qarhõnian people|Qarhõnians]] and other minority ethno-linguistic groups have their own cultures and traditions, although heavily influenced by Qymish culture. [[Litagẽyan culture|Litagẽyan]], [[Qarhõnian culture|Qarhõnian]] and [[Rumykian culture]] especially are considered more distinct due to belonging to the [[North Ottelic languages|North Ottelic]] and [[South Sukatic languages|South Sukatic]] traditions, while Qymish, Emadinian and Sunamese are all [[North Sukatic languages|North Sukatic]]. 

