===Comics===
{{Main|Qymish comics}}
[[File:Ganzlo_comics.jpg|200px|thumb|right|[[Ganzlo]] and [[Miss Ilody]], the main characters of the comics [[Ganzlo (comics)|Ganzlo]], based on [[Bacha XVII of Qym|King Bacha XVII]] and [[Queen Zvonica of Qym|Queen Zvonica]].]]
[[Qymish comics]] are well-ingrained in Qymish culture. 

One of the most popular comics is [[Ganzlo (comics)|Ganzlo]], a spoof of [[Bacha XVII of Qym|King Bacha XVII]] had he not been born into royalty. It was inspired by his early adulthood blunders, such as his {{abbr|2356|1957 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} car crash at the [[University of Zhol]]. The series was created in {{abbr|2358|1959 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} and features an office worker named [[Ganzlo]] working for a newspaper, a big slacker who commits all sorts of blunders every week, as numerous as they are varied: explosions, sabotage of business contracts, inventions of unproven machines that cause disasters, etc. Initially, the comics generated much controversy and was decried for mocking the royal family, but [[Bacha XVII of Qym|Bacha XVII]]'s personal approval of the comic resulted in it becoming a cult classic. Ganzlo's main love interest in the comics is a character named [[Miss Ilody]], who is based on [[Queen Zvonica of Qym|Queen Zvonica]].

==Architecture and housing==
{{Main|Architecture of Qym}}

Many renown architectural structures in Qym have royal functions, such as the art deco and neoclassical [[Palace of Zhol]], built during the {{abbr|2320s|1930s (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, {{abbr|2330s|1930s/1940s (Earth Year Equivalent)}} and {{abbr|2340s|1940s (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, or the gothic, baroque and rococo [[Palace of Zhifol]]. 

In terms of residential architecture, wall-to-wall [[wikipedia:Terraced house|row houses]], duplexes and triplexes dominate nation-wide in most cities, and generally comprise alleyways and balconies on two to three stories. The staircases of duplexes and triplexes are a unique architectural feature in Qymish cities. Primarily located outside, with a separate entrance at the front, the railings are made of wrought iron and the steps of wood. Their design varies considerably from one building to another. Their origins date back to the {{abbr|mid 22nd century|late 19th century (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, when urban population increased due to the influx of people from rural areas seeking work. This growth concerned the royal authorities, which adopted a law requiring property owners to maintain a small green space in front of their homes, providing more open space to the streets and neighborhoods. This law led to the design of new housing: narrow, deep apartments on two or three floors, built side-by-side. These are less expensive to heat in winter compared to those with a shared interior space, since the staircase is located outside, above the small green spaces created at the front. 

In larger cities and closer to city centers, such as in [[Zhol]], apartment buildings are common and vary in style from wall-to-wall stone or brick tenements above five stories to individual high-rise towers made of concrete or steel and glass. [[wikipedia:Large-panel-system building|Large-panel-system buildings]] are common in newer neighborhoods of larger cities, usually built to house low-income tenants. 

In rural areas, single-family homes are the norm due to the larger available space and lower demand for housing. Traditional Qymish rural houses are rectangular, single-story structures with high, steep roofs. This roof design serves, among other things, to prevent snow accumulation in winter. Traditional houses are generally built of wood or stone, while modern houses are built in a wood, steel or concrete frame, with brick, stucco, vinyl, aluminium or fibrocement exterior cladding. 

