==Society==
===Family and children===
The typical Qymish family structure is that of a [[wikipedia:Neolocal residence|neolocal]] [[wikipedia:nuclear family|nuclear family]] comprising parents and children. [[wikipedia:Extended family|Extended families]] living in the same household do exist but are less common, either as [[wikipedia:Matrilocal residence|matrilocal]] or [[wikipedia:Patrilocal residence|patrilocal residency]]. Historically, Qym was a patrilocal society and it was expected for the wife to go live with her husband's family. However, this changed with economic development, the industrial revolution, and rapid urbanization in the {{abbr|early 24th century|1911 to 1952 (Earth Years Equivalent)}}. As in most developed countries, nuclear family became prevalent in the {{abbr|second half of the 24th century|1952 to 1994 (Earth Years Equivalent)}}. 
[[File:Children_playing_street_hockey_in_Byvichek_in_2374.jpg|250px|thumb|right|Children playing [[wikipedia:Shinny|street hockey]] in [[Byvichek]] in {{abbr|2374|1972 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, using snow as a makeshift goal.]]
As more Qymish women work (70% in labor force compared to 80% for men), careers are increasingly prioritized over having children, while other factors are also in play, such as risk aversion to bad partners, complicated pregnancies or bad experiences giving birth, individualism, cost of living, popular culture, and the rise of online dating and relationships. As of {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, more Qymish people are single than in any other time in its history, and the total fertility rate (TFR) dropped significantly to 1.56 after rebounding to 1.74 in the {{abbr|2410s|2002 to 2010 (Earth Years Equivalent)}}. While the Qymish family is usually portrayed by popular media as being two parents with two children, there are many cases of couples opting to have only one child, the so-called [[wikipedia:only child|one-child]] households, allowing more resources – financial or temporal – to be allocated to the offspring. [[wikipedia:Voluntary childlessness|Voluntary childlessness]] and the [[wikipedia:Singleton (lifestyle)|singleton]] lifestyle are also increasingly common. 

Children in Qym have certain rights limited [[Legal age in Qym|based on their age]]. They are notably required to attend [[Education in Qym|compulsory education]] from ages {{abbr|7|5.8 (Earth Age Equivalent)}} to {{abbr|20|16.6 (Earth Age Equivalent)}}. They are also [[wikipedia:Age of criminal responsibility|criminally responsible]] from the age of {{abbr|14|11.7 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, which is lower than in most countries. Under Qymish law, the [[wikipedia:age of majority|age of majority]] is {{abbr|20|16.6 (Earth Age Equivalent)}}, after which most age limits no longer apply. However, age limits remain with regard to [[wikipedia:List of minimum driving ages|driving]] ({{abbr|21|17.5 (Earth Age Equivalent)}} for cars, {{abbr|24|20 (Earth Age Equivalent)}} for trucks), [[wikipedia:gambling age|gambling]] ({{abbr|24|20 (Earth Age Equivalent)}}), [[wikipedia:marriageable age|marriage]] ({{abbr|24|20 (Earth Age Equivalent)}}), [[wikipedia:Age of consent|sexual consent]] ({{abbr|24|20 (Earth Age Equivalent)}} with lower restrictions; see [[age of consent in Qym]]), [[wikipedia:Age of candidacy|candidacy]] ({{abbr|30|25 (Earth Age Equivalent)}}) and [[wikipedia:Retirement age|retirement]] ({{abbr|75|62 (Earth Age Equivalent)}}). 

Youth culture in Qym is characterized by an active outdoor presence with limited to no parental supervision, reflecting a [[wikipedia:High-trust and low-trust societies|high-trust society]]. [[wikipedia:Shinny|Street hockey]] is a common amateur youth sport in any season, while organized youth leagues of most sports exist throughout the country. [[wikipedia:Free-range parenting|Free-range parenting]] remains the norm, although Qymish law stipulates children under {{abbr|12|10 (Earth Age Equivalent)}} must not be left alone without adult supervision. Modern parenting problems increasingly affecting Qymish families include [[wikipedia:Helicopter parent|helicopter parents]], [[wikipedia:Narcissistic parent|narcissistic parents]], and [[wikipedia:iPad kid|iPad kids]]. 

===Women in Qym===
{{Main|Women in Qym}}
{{See|Feminism in Qym}}

To this day, Qym remains a [[wikipedia:patriarchy|patriarchy]]. Only male descendants can [[Line of succession to the Qymish throne|inherit the Qymish throne]] and become the [[King of Qym]], while only men could inherent property for most of the country's history. As such, the symbol of absolute power in the country had always been a man. In religious and folkloric mythology, this has also been the case with the most important Qymish hero being a man: [[Sluj]]. Despite that reality, women had significant roles in Qymish history and mythology, albeit not in the foreground. U/C
[[File:Girls ice hockey team 1921.jpg|250px|thumb|left|A Qymish women's hockey team in {{abbr|2313|1921 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}]]
Feminism in Qym began in the {{abbr|23rd century|1827 to 1911 (Earth Years Equivalent)}} during the industrial revolution and was closely linked with [[wikipedia:Labor rights|labor]] and [[wikipedia:Civil and political rights|political rights]]. As such, it was often grouped together with [[Republicanism in Qym|republicanism]] and labeled as traitorous or promoting foreign ideologies. Despite the early difficulties, women's civic rights improved gradually over the next decades. Under [[Bacha XIII of Qym|King Bacha XIII]], women were allowed to form associations and community organizations, just like men. His successor, [[Bacha XIV of Qym|King Bacha XIV]], made a series of educational reforms, with both boys and girls now required to receive elementary education. By the [[Fifteenth Bachan era]], at the dawn of the {{abbr|24th century|1911 to 1994 (Earth Years Equivalent)}}, women had the same property and inheritance rights as men, but remained under the obligation to obey her husband. Despite an increasing number of women participating in the labor force, a woman's primary role was understood to be at home, taking care of her family up until the {{abbr|2360s|1960s (Earth Years Equivalent)}}. 
[[File:Salora electronics factory in Salo, in South-Western Finland, in 1973 (JOKAVKH2E09-7).tif|250px|thumb|right|Qymish women working in a factory in {{abbr|2375|1973 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}]]
Following the accession to the throne of [[Bacha XVII of Qym|King Bacha XVII]] in {{abbr|2372|1970 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, women's rights improved rapidly. The obligation of the wife to obey her husband was removed by royal decree that same year, while discriminatory provisions resulting from matrimonial regimes and from other legal regulations were also removed. In {{abbr|2375|1973 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, married women obtained equal rights with husbands with regard to legal authority over their children during marriage, abolishing the concept of paternal authority, which gave the husband more legal rights with regard to judicial matters concerning the children, and replacing it with that of parental authority shared equally between the wife and husband. Other reforms based on gender equality followed throughout the {{abbr|2380s|1977 to 1985 (Earth Years Equivalent)}}, including the introduction of the concept of family patrimony, ensuring financial equality between spouses when a marriage ends, and also equal pay for women who did the same work as men. 

Until {{abbr|2372|1970 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, Qym did not have a divorce law, and spouses could only end their marriage if they obtained a private royal decree. From a religious standpoint, within [[Scildism]], divorce was seen as a union of man and woman for humanity and against the gods, reflected in the epic of [[Sluj]] and his wife [[Halob]], in ''[[The King who defied the Gods]]''. Divorce has thus historically been regarded as caving in to the gods' discord and contrary to humanity's cooperative nature. In a television interview, [[Bacha XVII of Qym|King Bacha XVII]] explained his decision to legalize divorce by stating it was "only natural and human to change one's mind or have different circumstances than when you married". He added a divorced required just as much interpersonal cooperation as a marriage, if not more, and so was perfectly in line with [[Scildism]]. Nowadays, Qym maintains a low marriage rate but also a low divorce rate, with cohabitation increasingly becoming the preferred form of living arrangement for a couple. Under Qymish law, cohabitation relationships confer legal rights and obligations on couples who are not formally married. In such situations, they are considered ''de facto'' unions while marriage are ''de jure'' unions. 

While Qym ranks high in most indexes related to gender equality, Qymish women continue to face numerous systemic challenges. Domestic violence is a serious problem, as are obstacles to the timely prosecution of rape, the prevalence of sexual harassment, and a persistent gender income gap, among other iniquities. 

===Romantic partnerships and sexuality===
{{See|LGBTQ rights in Qym}}

Despite advancements in [[LGBTQ rights in Qym|LGBTQ rights]] in the last decades, Qym remains a [[wikipedia:Heteronormativity|heteronormative society]]. 



===Work===
{{Main|Qymish labor law}}
