Standard working hours are {{abbr|24.5|41.17 hours (Earth 7-days weekly equivalent)}} per {{abbr|week|A Basis week is 10 days of 10 hours, and a Basis hour is equal to 2.2 Earth hours}}, whereas Qymish workers typically work 7 out of 10 weekly days. The [[wikipedia:legal working age|legal working age]] is {{abbr|20|16.6 (Earth Age Equivalent)}}, meaning minors below that age cannot work legally. In practice, this is often ignored in [[wikipedia:small business|small businesses]] like [[wikipedia:convenience store|convenience stores]] where children are usually expected to help out their parents run the family store. After his enthronement in {{abbr|2443|2029.46 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, [[Qodzar of Qym|King Qodzar]] announced a gradual process of reducing the standard working hours to {{abbr|14|23.53 hours (Earth 7-days weekly equivalent)}} per {{abbr|week|A Basis week is 10 days of 10 hours, and a Basis hour is equal to 2.2 Earth hours}}, citing the average work week already being {{abbr|17.45|29.33 hours (Earth 7-days weekly equivalent)}} and also the benefits to employment, carbon emissions, well-being, gender equality, family care, and the increase in free time. This announcement was met positively by the Qymish public, according to polls, but quite negatively by business owners and, as a result, generated negative media coverage. 

A standard work week depends on the profession but usually comprises the remaining 7 days of a normal week. As such, Qymish people usually work from the 4th day to the 10th day, based on the [[Bachan calendar]]. The first three days, known as the '''weekstart'', are set aside for chores, personal work, or leisure. Traditional Qymish business hours, that is when a store is open or a business has its workers working, are {{abbr|3:50|8:24 AM (Earth Time Equivalent)}} to {{abbr|7:00|4:48 PM (Earth Time Equivalent)}}. Including overtime, the maximum working hours cannot exceed {{abbr|28 hours per week|47 hours per week (Earth 7-days weekly equivalent)}} and {{abbr|4 hours per day|8.8 (Earth Nominal Hours Equivalent) or 6.7 (Earth Day-Length-Adjusted Hours Equivalent)}}. Qymish labor law regulates the minimum working hours: part-time jobs should not allow for less than {{abbr|10.5 hours per week|17.65 hours per week (Earth 7-days weekly equivalent)}} without a workplace collective agreement. These agreements can allow for less, under specific conditions. 

A lunchtime break is typical and usually about {{abbr|half an hour|66 minutes (Earth Time Equivalent)}}. Employers often allow employees to split that lunchtime break into segments, enabling them to take other breaks during their workday. Non-regulated pauses during the workday for natural bodily functions such as pee breaks or menstrual hygiene are not permitted to be documented as working time. Qymish labor law also rewards loyalty and employment stability by automatically granting raises based on the length of employment and changes in the cost-of-living, with the aim of maintaining stable [[wikipedia:real wages|real wage]] growth. This particularity of Qymish work culture has been criticized for discouraging workers from asking for merit-based raises and for employers to grant them. On the other hand, it has also been praised for standardizing pays and reducing gender pay gaps, as women are less likely to ask for raises. 

===Etiquette===
====Greetings====
[[File:Vuistje.jpg|250px|thumb|right|The [[wikipedia:fist bump|fist bump]] is the main form of greeting in Qym.]]
Qymish people do not usually shake hands when greeting strangers, as holding hands in any way or form is considered an intimate practice reserved for loved ones and family. Instead, they lightly [[wikipedia:fist bump|bump their fists]] against one another, a practice popularized through the sport of hockey in the {{abbr|23rd century|1828 to 1911 (Earth Years Equivalent)}}. A cursory bow of the head is usually sufficient in more formal situations when meeting strangers, for instance when entering a shop or being formally introduced to someone. When greeting close friends or family members, people hug and kiss once on each cheeks, regardless of gender. Hugging and kissing someone who is only an acquaintance or worse, a stranger, is taboo and may be considered sexual harassment under modern Qymish law.

====Eating and drinking====
In a typical family meal or among friends, the members gather at a table and sit only when invited to sit by the host. The seating arrangement is also at the discretion of the host and it is considered polite as a guest to ask the host where to sit. Furthermore, guests are not to eat or drink unless given permission by the host while at the table. It is customary to wait for the host and/or cook to sit and finish serving food before then making a toast in their honor or to thank them for the food, after which, and only then, is it allowed to eat. 

With the advent of the radio, television and smartphones, technology at the table has raised issues. It is often to the discretion of the people involved, but in general, most Qymish people frown upon people using their smartphone while eating, for any reason besides an emergency situation. Technology use at a table is only allowed when a person is eating alone, for example at a restaurant. 

When it comes to paying the bill in a restaurant or elsewhere, gratuity is not expected and is instead considering insulting. It is only allowed in certain circumstances, when returning money would be inconvenient or insignificant, such as when the buyer rounds up the price of their purchase when paying in cash. 

Making noise when drinking or eating is considered rude and impolite, particularly chewing with one's mouth open, slurping and burping. 

====Removal of footwear indoors====
In Qym, it is customary to take one's shoes off at the entryway before entering a house. Some households may use indoor slippers, but it is far more common to walk around barefoot or in socks. Entering a house with shoes on is considered a major taboo, as it is synonymous with bringing in outside dirt and snow to one's residence, making it messier. Indeed, in winter, footwear worn outside is often wet from snow and soiled by the associated salt/sand that is used to clear roads and sidewalks of snow cover. Consequently, all schools in Qym require students to bring with them or leave behind a pair of "indoor shoes" at the school for indoor wear during the winter months. The same rules are applied in gyms, offices, and other establishments during the winter months. During other seasonal periods, it is common to wear outdoor shoes to school or work. An exception to this are public places like restaurants and stores, where it is not expected for clients to remove their shoes, regardless of the season and snow coverage. That is because these businesses are usually equipped with hard floors that are easy to clean or carpets designed to absorb melted snow. 

====Funerals====
[[File:Bone_sculpture_in_the_catacombs_of_Teurek.jpg|250px|thumb|right|Bone sculpture in the [[Catacombs of Teurek]].]]
As a predominantly [[Scildism|Scildist]] country, Qymish funeral traditions are similar to the rest of [[Meran]] and comprise two burials. During the initial burial of the body in a burial chamber, the body is left to decompose for about a year to obtain a bare skeleton. During the secondary burial, the deceased's family gathers the bones, which are then piled up in bulk before being stacked in [[wikipedia:ossuary|ossuaries]]. Once an ossuary is full, the bones are transferred to a common grave during a second funeral service to which the entire local community is invited. 
[[File:Man and Woman at Dia De Los Muertos (49275956137).jpg|250px|thumb|left|A young Qymish couple during the [[Festival of the Dead]] in {{abbr|2431|2019 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}.]]
Scildist ossuaries are often decorated with the bones of deceased people, making them museums of the dead. Due to lack of space, most cities have [[wikipedia:catacombs|catacombs]], which are underground ossuaries. Catacombs, although most notable as underground passageways and ossuaries, also house many decorations and sculptures dedicated to the dead. The [[Catacombs of Zhifol]], beneath the [[Palace of Zhifol]], are particularly famous for being the resting place of all the members of the [[House of Zhifol]], the ruling royal family of Qym. Other famous catacombs in the country include the [[Catacombs of Zhol]], by far the largest, and also the [[Catacombs of Teurek]] for its unique decorative ornaments. 

During the [[Festival of the Dead]], Scildists perform an evening communal procession to the local ossuary with candles and various skull-related iconography, from makeup, masks or baked goods shaped like skulls. This festival honors all the deceased and a person's ancestors, making it one of the most important in [[Scildism]]. Specific regional varieties regarding this festival vary throughout Basis, but it is generally considered a continuation of funeral practices and a way to respect the deceased long after their death. 

Among the sizable [[Sunneism|Sunneist]] community (around 6 million people or 3.7% of the population), funerals differ considerably. To them, a corpse is considered dirty and of no value to the gods or the living. As such, the corpses are left in roofless structures outside of towns called [[NAME]] to rot while exposed to [[Sunne]] and [[Mōna]], and left to the animals who feast on them. This [[wikipedia:excarnation|excarnation]] is then followed by the cremation of the remains to remove all impurities, a funeral rite known as [[NAME]]. 

Other minority religious groups have difference funeral practices. To the [[Ānism|Ānists]], a corpse must be treated with utmost respect to not insult God who created humans in his own image. Consequently, specific funeral rites are to be followed. The corpse must first be bathed, completely nude, followed by embalming and prayer. The corpse is then enshrouded and buried in a cemetery. The corpse must face upward, towards the sky and God, and burying a corpse with its face towards the ground is a great insult. 

====Directness====
Qymish people are very direct and do not shy away from confrontation. They value honesty, clarity and loyalty, leading them to say what they think without filtering or sugar-coating. As such, Qymish people are often regarded as blunt and at times aggressive by foreigners. Indirect or overly cautious speech might be interpreted literally and lead to confusion, or as an expression of weakness and indecisiveness. 
[[File:Chi-wbs14 rd (39732309584).jpg|thumb|right|A [[wikipedia:Fighting in ice hockey|ice hockey fight]] between two players while a referee watches on.]]
The honesty in Qymish directness means that feedback and opinions are often expressed more frankly. In Qymish culture, it's typical and expected to deliver bad news or criticism gently but clearly, rather than beat around the bush. As long as there is politeness, Qymish people value when people speak openly about what is important to them. This approach can be surprising to those from cultures where feedback is heavily softened. For instance, a Qymish person might say in a work meeting "your work has several errors that need to be fixed" whereas a foreign colleague would say "you did well, but there are a few minor issues to address" even if they did not do well at all. 

Whereas some cultures might require a person to go in a roundabout and overly polite way to ask someone to open a window, for example saying "Excuse me, would you mind opening the window, if that's not too much trouble?", a Qymish person would simply say in a polite tone "Hey, can you open the window?" and add a quick "Thanks" at the end if the request is accepted. To Qymish people, adding cushion or sugar-coating phrases is being dishonest and cowardly. Tone is therefore very important when communicating, as it indicates a person's true emotions and level of respect for the person they are talking to. 

The Qymish sport of [[wikipedia:ice hockey|hockey]] is a good representation of Qymish directness. In that sport, players are allowed to [[wikipedia:Fighting in ice hockey|fist fight each other]] but must respect [[wikipedia:Fighting_in_ice_hockey#Etiquette|a certain etiquette]] to ensure the fight is fair and clean, such as [[wikipedia:Fighting_in_ice_hockey#Etiquette|dropping their gloves]] to agree to the fight, a practice whose origin lies in medieval Qym, where throwing a glove to the ground before an offender indicated a challenge to a [[wikipedia:duel|duel]]. Likewise, players often [[wikipedia:trash talk|chirp]] at each other, as do referees, leading to funny [[wikipedia:Conversation#Banter|banter]] and entertaining mic'd up moments for the fans. 

Mirroring this hockey culture, [[wikipedia:Street fighting|street fighting]] is tolerated in Qymish society, with penalties often merely minor fines for disruption of public order if the fighting is done in a public place, and usually results in no penalties if it is [[wikipedia:mutual combat|mutual combat]] on a private property. If any severe injuries are caused during a fight, the severity of the injury will impact the penalty of the participants and may result in prison time. 

===Pets===
Like elsewhere on Basis, cats and dogs are the most common pets owned by Qymish people. U/C

Common dog breeds originating from Qym include the [[wikipedia:Mudi|Mudi]], [[wikipedia:Karelo-Finnish Laika|Roduvas]], and the [[wikipedia:Schnauzer|Vusat]]. Similarly, common Qymish cat breeds include the [[wikipedia:Somali cat|Sandy cat]], [[wikipedia:Russian Blue|Nagurian Blue]], and [[wikipedia:Snowshoe cat|Snowshoe cat]]. 

