===Traditional clothes===
{{Main|National costumes of Qym}}

Qym does not have a specific national costume but rather several regional ones. These share some common characteristics, however, such as the use of [[wikipedia:Hood (headgear)|hoods]] ([[Qymish language|Qymish]]: ⰽⰰⱂⱖⱋ, ''kapüsh''), [[wikipedia:scarf|scarfs]] ([[Qymish language|Qymish]]: ⱇⱆⰾⰰⱃ, ''fular'') and [[wikipedia:Knit cap|knit caps]] ([[Qymish language|Qymish]]: ⱅⱖⰽ, ''tük''), as well as jackets for men and dresses for women. The designs of civil clothes were developed from ancient times, to [[Byrakans|Byrakan]] then [[Ottelians|Ottelian]], and later under [[Sukats|Sukat]] influence, and in cities along the [[SEA]] coast, primarily under other [[Meran]]ian influence. 

The [[wikipedia:fez|fez]] was a popular headwear for the upper class starting in the early {{abbr|2190s|1820s (Earth Years Equivalent)}}, owing to its adoption by [[Bacha XII of Qym|King Bacha XII]] on his official portrait. Subsequent kings also wore a fez for their official portrait until [[Bacha XVII of Qym|King Bacha XVII]] who opted not to do so in {{abbr|2372|1970 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}. By then, the practice had become outdated throughout [[Meran]], especially from the {{abbr|2340s|1944 to 1952 (Earth Years Equivalent)}} onward. The [[March of Litagẽ]] continues to wear a rounded white fez as part of their traditional clothes. 

Traditional costumes are nowadays only worn during regional or national festivals, for theater performances, or during the shooting of a period movie or television series. 

===Modern fashion===
[[Zhol]] is one of the fashion centers on Basis and host several luxury brands' headquarters. The Qymish royal family has often been a trend-setter in modern-day Qymish fashion. The styles of [[Queen Zvonica of Qym|Queen Zvonica]] and [[Queen Qümica of Qym|Queen Qümica]] have particularly attracted media attention not just in Qym but also throughout Basis. Several fashion subcultures originate in Qym, notably [[wikipedia:punk subculture|punk]] ([[Qymish language|Qymish]]: ⱂⱑⰽ) and [[wikipedia:goth subculture|goth]] ([[Qymish language|Qymish]]: ⰳⱁⱉ). Traditional fashion has has also seen a certain revival with the neo-traditional sub-culture, popularized by period television series, characterizing the nostalgic fashion of the {{abbr|2430s|2019 to 2027 (Earth Years Equivalent)}}. Niche and microtrends have also been popularized by social media influences and the internet. 

The modern [[wikipedia:hoddie|hoodie]] is generally considered to have originated in Qym in the {{abbr|2320s|1927.5 to 1935.7 (Earth Years Equivalent)}}, although that claim is disputed. It was usually worn by working-class laborers in colder environments. Nowadays, hoodies are a popular clothing item and are often worn under a coat or jacket to provide an extra layer of clothing in the winter. 

The [[Zhol Fashion Week]], held bi-annually in the [[Bachan calendar|3rd]] and the [[Bachan calendar|7th months]], is a series of events where buyers, the press, and the general public are shown international fashion collections. Most of the events have limited admission by invitation only. Attendees are usually other designers, [[House of Zhifol|members of the royal family]], celebrities, and social media influencers. The fashion week has often been the location of protests, including environmental protests and [[wikipedia:Fur_clothing#Anti-fur_campaigns|anti-fur]] activists.

==Cuisine==
{{Main|Cuisine of Qym}}
[[File:Pain aux raisins 02.jpg|250px|thumb|right|[[wikipedia:Pain aux raisins|Bavozian bread]], a type of raisin roll pastry commonly eaten for breakfast.]]
Owing to its abundant wheat and rye fields, [[wikipedia:bread|bread]] is the staple of Qymish cuisine, with over 5,000 different types of bread baked daily in Qym, along with over 2,000 different types of pastries and rolls. It is served usually for breakfast (toasts, rolls or pastries) and lunch (sandwiches). 

Qym has important [[wikipedia:Poultry farming|poultry]], [[wikipedia:Pig farming|pork]] and [[wikipedia:Cuniculture|cuniculture]] industries. Qymish people are the biggest consumers of [[wikipedia:rabbit|rabbit]], [[wikipedia:marmot|marmot]] and [[wikipedia:Venison|venison]] ([[wikipedia:deer|deer]]) meats on [[Basis]]. [[Sive]], a working-class rabbit stew, has been popular since medieval times due to its low cost. [[Fried rabbit]] is also common, especially as a fast food dish in cities. Other popular dishes include [[wikipedia:Kreplach|tazhof]] dumplings, [[wikipedia:Ghapama|rëkuvy]], a stuffed pumpkin dish, [[wikipedia:rösti|rösti]], similar to [[wikipedia:Hash browns|hash browns]], and the [[wikipedia:Montreal hot dog|rotö]] (literally ''burper''), a sausage served in a partially sliced bun. 

The [[County of Nagur]] has a specialized regional cuisine mainly based on [[wikipedia:seafood|seafood]]. Other regional cuisines exist throughout Qym. 

==Sports==
{{Main|Sports in Qym}}
[[File:240106 MN Montreal JohnMc037 (53446848607).jpg|250px|thumb|right|A professional women's league game of [[wikipedia:ice hockey|ice hockey]], which is by far the most popular sport in Qym.]]
The most popular sport in terms of spectators is [[wikipedia:ice hockey|ice hockey]], commonly referred simply as "hockey" (ⱝⱁⰽⰺ ''hoky''). Ice hockey is native to Qym and considered its [[wikipedia:national sport|national sport]]. It was practiced on frozen lakes or rivers in winter for centuries with no fixed set of rules or equipment. The modern rules were established in {{abbr|2257|1875 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} by students at the [[University of Zhol]]. Temporary ice rinks are set up in most city parks every winter throughout the country, allowing kids and adults alike to skate or play hockey freely. Other winter sports are popular, including [[wikipedia:sledding|sledding]] [[wikipedia:skiing|skiing]], [[wikipedia:snowboarding|snowboarding]], [[wikipedia:snowmobile|snowmobiling]], [[wikipedia:figure skating|figure skating]], [[wikipedia:ringette|ringette]], [[wikipedia:broomball|broomball]], and [[wikipedia:curling|curling]]. The latter four and hockey can also be played indoors in [[wikipedia:ice hockey arena|arenas]] during warmer seasons. 
[[File:Aryna Sabalenka vs. Qinwen Zheng in a quarterfinals of the 2024 US Open - 01.jpg|250px|thumb|left|The [[Qymish Open]] tennis tournament. Tennis is the second most watched sport after hockey.]]
In terms of more mainstream sports in warmer weather, Qym follows global trends with [[wikipedia:association football|football]], [[wikipedia:basketball|basketball]], [[wikipedia:rugby|rugby]], and [[wikipedia:tennis|tennis]] commonly practiced. [[wikipedia:Cycling|Cycling]], [[wikipedia:running|running]] and [[wikipedia:swimming|swimming]] are the most common sports by number of regular practitioners. Men tend to cycle more while women tend to swim more, while both genders run about the same. Hockey is the leading sport among young adults aged {{abbr|20|16.6 (Earth Age Equivalent)}} to {{abbr|30|25 (Earth Age Equivalent)}}, with a participation rate of 27.3%. It is followed by football, which holds a participation rate of 12.8%, while tennis ranks third with a rate of 11.2%. Football is the leading sport among Qymish minors, with 15% of those aged {{abbr|4|3.3 (Earth Age Equivalent)}} to {{abbr|20|16.6 (Earth Age Equivalent)}} participating in organized football. 

According to a {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} survey on sports watching, 46% of [[Qymish people]] indicated a preference for watching hockey, 10% stated that they neither watch nor follow sports, 18% expressed a preference for tennis, and 16% favored football. A gender analysis revealed that 73% of men opted for one of the leading three sports hockey, football or tennis, whereas 65% of women did so. A similar number of women enjoyed watching hockey and tennis compared to men, but men were more likely to watch football. This may be due to the greater success of women leagues and athletes in hockey and tennis. The [[Qymish Hockey League]] (QHL) garners the most audience, while the [[Qymish Open]], held in [[Zhol]], is one of the annual Grand Slam tennis tournaments. 

Motorsport is also popular in Qym, particularly with Qymish men. The country hosts two [[wikipedia:Formula One|Formula One]] races annually, the [[Qymish Grand Prix]] in [[Nagur]] and the [[Trinational Grand Prix]] partially held in [[Teurek]]. Many Formula One drivers are Qymish, including many champions over history. 

==Symbols==
{{Main|National symbols of Qym}}

Four animals are emblematic of Qym, and notably present in [[Coat of arms of Qym|its coat of arms]]: the [[wikipedia:Eastern gray squirrel|gray squirrel]] (symbol of [[House Zhifol]]), the [[wikipedia:raccoon|raccoon]], the [[wikipedia:rabbit|rabbit]], and the [[wikipedia:hawk|hawk]]. Other prominent symbols include the national motto, "[[Follow the King]]", the sports of [[wikipedia:ice hockey|ice]] and [[wikipedia:street hockey|street hockey]], and natural features such as [[Mount Synil]] and [[Lake Aquly]]. 

Qym has ten official and symbolic public holidays, starting with the [[?? Day]] on DAY MONTH, [[Queen's Day]] on DAY MONTH, [[?? Day]] on DAY MONTH, [[?? Day]] on DAY MONTH, [[Basis Day]] on DAY MONTH, [[wikipedia:Labour Day|Labor Day]] on DAY MONTH, [[Summer/Winter Solstice]] on DAY MONTH, [[King's Day]] on DAY MONTH, [[Winter/Summer Solstice]] on DAY MONTH, and the [[Last Day of the Year]].

==Tourism==
{{Main|Tourism in Qym}}

[[wikipedia:Tourism|Tourism]] is an important industry in Qym and is influenced by the attractiveness of Qymish culture to foreigners and residents alike. Despite being a country that imports tourism more than it exports, that is to say spending by residents traveling abroad exceeds that of foreign visitors, Qym is the [[Basis Tourism rankings|fifth-most-visited country on Basis]] with 54.7 million international tourist arrivals in {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}. Meanwhile, based on international tourism revenue (i.e. exports, in terms of goods and services), Qym ranked third globally that year with receipts of $93.6 billion, while it ranked second in tourism expenditure (i.e. imports, in terms of Qymish tourist expenditure abroad), at $152.4 billion. [[Zhol]] was the [[List of cities on Basis by international visitors|sixth-most-visited city on Basis]] in {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, with 19.3 million arrivals. The majority of tourists (around 80%) come from neighboring countries in [[Meran]], notably from [[COUNTRY1]] (NUM%), [[COUNTRY2]] (NUM%), [[COUNTRY3]] (NUM%), [[COUNTRY4]] (NUM%), and [[COUNTRY5]] (NUM%). This geographical distribution highlights the predominant role of continental tourism in Qym's international visitor numbers. 

Other than [[Zhol]], the most visited cities are [[Teurek]], [[Patsalys]], [[Nagur]], [[Prasty]], and [[Byvichek]]. Meanwhile, the most visited tourist sites are the [[Old City of Zhol]], the [[Palace of Zhol]], the [[Palace of Patsalys]], the [[Cave of Sluj]] in [[Prasty]], the [[Boreal War Memorial (Teurek)|Boreal War Memorial]] in [[Teurek]], the [[Royal Museum of Art (Qym)|Royal Museum of Art]] in [[Byvichek]], the [[Catacombs of Teurek]], the [[Royal Museum of Innovation (Qym)|Royal Museum of Innovation]] in [[Nagur]], the [[Catacombs of Zhol]], the [[Royal Museum of War (Qym)|Royal Museum of War]] in [[Zhol]], and the [[Royal Museum of Culture (Qym)|Royal Museum of Culture]] in [[Patsalys]]. 

The [[Scildism|Scildist]] [[Festival of the Dead]] also attracts considerable tourists due to its carnival-like celebrations, which has generated backlash from elements of the Qymish public who decry the commercialization of the yearly religious mourning festival. [[Bacha XVIII of Qym|King Bacha XVIII]] notably referred to the festival as a "consumer-driven spectacle". [[wikipedia:Tourismphobia|Anti-tourist sentiment]] is also rising, especially in older parts of major cities, such as in the [[Old City of Zhol]], where attacks on tourists have been reported in recent years. The main causes are generally understood to be a perception of disrespectful behavior by foreign tourists towards Qymish culture or people, ignorance of Qymish laws and customs, crowdedness, increased littering, and gentrification of urban centers due to rising housing costs and the proliferation of hotels and short-term apartment rentals catering to tourists. 

Parallelly, the behavior of Qymish tourists abroad has been criticized in many countries, notably for assuming certain atypical Qymish customs are applicable throughout Basis, such as [[wikipedia:street fighting|street fighting]], [[wikipedia:drinking in public|public drinking]], [[wikipedia:inappropriateness|indecent clothing]], [[wikipedia:public urination|public urination]], and [[wikipedia:public display of affection|public displays of affection]]. Qymish tourists have also been accused of engaging with and being responsible for the proliferation of [[wikipedia:sex tourism|sex tourism]] and [[wikipedia:prostitution|prostitution]] in lower income countries such as [[Haynadur]]. 

==Sociological issues==
===Hypermasculinity and hyperfemininity===
Hypermasculinity, refers to the unhealthy promotion of the "[[wikipedia:machismo|macho]] personality" and is often classified as synonymous with [[wikipedia:toxic masculinity|toxic masculinity]]. In the field of [[wikipedia:clinical psychology|clinical psychology]], this term has been used since {{abbr|2388|1984 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} and is defined as consisting of a callous sexual attitudes toward women, the belief that violence is manly, and that danger is exciting. Hypermasculinity also delves on emotional aspects of self-control, withdrawal and repression of feelings, whereas men are expected to be stoic and monitor their own emotions in order to maintain an image of manly toughness. Based on research by the Qymish sociologist [[Tomik Pavym]], a man expressing his emotions is seen, in a hypermasculine context, as unmanly, weak, childlike and feminine, prompting stigma from his peers and from women as well. Pavym's study shows that many Qymish men deliberately avoid behaviors and attitudes such as compassion and emotional expression, deeming these traits feminine and thus rejecting them altogether, leading to behaviors more akin to competition rather than connection with other people. 

Pavym claims the portrayal of men in mass media is the greatest factor explaining the phenomenon of hypermasculinity in Qymish society and throughout [[Basis]] as well. Whether in advertisement, gaming, movies or TV series, lead male characters almost always feature [[wikipedia:masculine beauty ideal|masculine beauty ideals]] and emotional elements of hypermasculine behavior such as toughness and stoicism. The omnipresence of hypermasculinity in Qymish media has led to the construction of a system of conscious or unconscious re-enactment of the values they perpetuate, which is done by both men and women according to Pavym. In other words, the media environment contributes to the normalization of hypermasculinity, resulting in negative effects on both genders. Women are taught that hypermasculine behavior is the norm, that "[[wikipedia:Boys Will Be Boys|boys will be boys]]", and women are thus pressured to accept their role as recipients of disrespectful, callous sexual and physically violent behavior from men. Meanwhile, men are pressured to meet these designated masculine criteria by the media, women and other men, which can lead to feelings of insecurity, inferiority, and general psychological distress if they are not met, resulting in mental health issues. 

Hyperfemininity is the mirror concept with regard to women, coined in {{abbr|2397|1991 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} by Qymish sociologist [[Moloqa Debe]], as the exaggerated adherence to stereotypical feminine gender roles. Debe argues that the label of hypermasculinity and toxic masculinity are problematic if left by themselves, creating an idea of masculinity in general as either toxic/hyper or healthy, and oversimplifying gender expression to a set of antiquated and stereotypical gender roles. She therefore coined the concept of hyperfemininity to balance these gender issues, and avoid the misperception that "only men have issues while women are perfect" and that feminists are uninterested in or hostile towards the lives of men. In her view, hyperfeminine behavior includes an over-reliance on men as providers and protectors, [[wikipedia:deference|passivity or submissiveness]], and acting as a "[[wikipedia:male gaze|watched]] [[wikipedia:objectification|object]]". 

===Sexism===
By global standard, Qym is one of the most advanced countries on [[Basis]] as it pertains to [[wikipedia:gender equality|gender equality]]. However, [[wikipedia:sexism|sexism]] remains a problem in Qymish society affecting men and women alike, although disproportionally so for women. Expressions of negative association with women, such as "running like a girl" or "crying like a girl", are often used to "correct" a boy's behavior into hypermasculine ideals which constitute a form of sexism. Furthermore, in heterosexual dating, it was a common social norm for much of Qymish history for men to ask women out on dates or men to propose marriage while women were not expected to initiate any romantic inclination. Similarly, the traditional practice was for a man to pay for everything during a romantic date, consequently putting unequal social and financial pressure on men. 

According to the Qymish sociologist [[Moloqa Debe]], these sexist norms also directly contributed to the objectification of women and seeing romance as a form of conquest or transaction. Challenges to these norms first appeared in the {{abbr|2340s|1944 to 1952 (Earth Years Equivalent)}} with the increased employment of women, and gradually resulted in emboldening women to initiate just like men, and also to [[wikipedia:Going Dutch|cover their own expenses during a date]], showcasing their financial independence. As such, gender egalitarian dating norms are common in modern Qymish society. 

Occupational sexism is the most prevalent form of sexism in Qym. Only a man can become the [[King of Qym|monarch]] according to the [[Fundamental Law of Qym]], which shows that at the highest and most symbolic level of occupation, men are deemed superior to women. There have been growing movements, even within the [[House of Zhifol]], to amend the succession law to include women, particularly during and since the [[Qymish succession crisis of 2440–2441]]. In terms of everyday people, there exist some hiring gaps related to gender identity, particularly when candidates apply for positions at a higher functional level and for male-dominated occupations. Furthermore, a [[wikipedia:Gender pay gap|gender pay gap]] persists despite Qymish labor law guaranteeing positional wage equality. 

Per [[Qymish naming customs]], it remains the norm for a child to take their father's surname rather than their mother's. Certain occupations are also widely dominated by a specific gender, such as [[wikipedia:firefighter|firefighting]] and the [[Qymish Armed Forces|armed forces]] for men due to the strict physical requirements. 

===Racism===
Racism is a significant issue. Ethnic minorities within Qym or from neighboring [[Meran]]ian countries have historically been the main target of discrimination, especially from the {{abbr|23rd century|1828 to 1911 (Earth Years Equivalent)}} onward, with the rise of [[Qymish nationalism]] and political tension with [[RIVAL NEIGHBOR]]. U/C

Increased immigration since the {{abbr|25th century|1994 to 2077 (Earth Years Equivalent)}} has brought more ethnic minorities to Qym, particularly from other continents with different [[wikipedia:human skin color|skin color]] and [[wikipedia:human physical appearance|physical characteristics]]. It is not uncommon for some Qymish people to see non-Meranian immigrants as "[[wikipedia:perpetual foreigner|perpetual foreigners]]" and not as truly "Qymish" due to their different physical appearance. 

[[Anti-transhumanism]] is often weaponized by Qymish media and commentators to attack [[Genonian people|people of Genonian descent]], fueling [[Ānismophobia]] and [[wikipedia:anti-Black racism|anti-Black racism]]. U/C

===Homophobia===
Homosexuality is legal and protected from discrimination under Qymish law. However, social stigma persist to this day, particularly towards what is perceived as controversial elements of [[wikipedia:gay culture|gay culture]] such as [[wikipedia:drag (entertainment)|drag]] and [[wikipedia:pride (LGBTQ culture)|pride]]. 

A {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} survey asked Qymish people whether homosexuality should be accepted in society. 60% of respondents said yes, while 30% said no. The remaining 10% were not sure. They also asked whether homosexual couples should be able to marry. Support for gay marriage was slightly lower at 45%, while those against represented 40% of respondents, with the remaining 15% unsure. Finally, the survey asked about same-sex adoption. Qymish respondents were 35% for, 45% against and 20% not sure. Same-sex couples are currently unable to marry or adopt, but same-sex [[wikipedia:unregistered cohabitation|unregistered cohabitation]] is recognized since {{abbr|2419|2008.7 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}. 

===Transphobia===
[[wikipedia:Transphobia|Transphobia]] is endemic in Qymish society, as trans people have no legal recognition or protection under Qymish law. [[Bacha XVIII of Qym|King Bacha XVIII]] has been outspoken about his disagreement with what he regards as "[[wikipedia:Anti-gender movement|gender theory]]", calling it a "growing threat", and declaring Qym was a [[wikipedia:cisgenderism|gender binary]] society. Such messages have been amplified by news reporting and social media, directly contributing to the rise of the gender-critical movement in [[Meran]] since the {{abbr|2420s|2010.5 to 2019 (Earth Years Equivalent)}}. Qymish transgender activists have criticized the King's concerns, asserting he was "demonizing an already vulnerable minority". Media personalities and psychologists have also made false claims regarding transgender people, which have spread into the population's consciousness. These include making unfounded parallels between [[wikipedia:transgender|transgenderism]] and [[transhumanism]], comparisons to [[wikipedia:otherkin|otherkin]], and continuously qualifying [[wikipedia:gender dysphoria|gender dysphoria]] as a mental illness. In a TV apperance in {{abbr|2436|2024 (Earth Year Equivalent)}}, [[Bacha XVIII of Qym|King Bacha XVIII]] explained: "What is it other than mental illness? They call it the [[wikipedia:gender dysphoria|gender identity disorder]] for a reason." Bacha then dismissed a correction that [[wikipedia:ICD-11|ICD-11]] uses the term [[wikipedia:gender incongruence|gender incongruence]] and does not consider it a mental illness, saying "they are fighting with semantics to justify something that lacks any common sense to you, me, and everyday people". 

According to a {{abbr|2440|2027 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} survey, 44.6% of Qymish trans adults reported being subjected to physical violence in public spaces, 73.6% reported receiving verbal abuse in public spaces, and 92.3% reported feeling unsafe in the country. 

===Violence===
According to research by the [[University of Zhol]], 42.6% of Qymish women and 27.9% of Qymish men experience intimate partner violence in their lifetime. The [[Qymish Center for Domestic Violence]] (QCDV) estimates that 10% of women and only 3% of men report cases of [[wikipedia:domestic violence|domestic violence]] against them to the police. Research has shown that domestic violence experienced by men is much more often physical than sexual, and that experienced by women is more often sexual than physical. A detailed analysis of victims' experiences by the QCDV shows that women experience a greater diversity of violent situations, report more frequently the potentially most serious types of violence, and experience these incidents for longer periods and with greater frequency than men. The estimated proportion of Qymish people reporting having witnessed violence (humiliation, physical violence) between their parents before the age of {{abbr|18|15 (Earth Age Equivalent)}} is 17.3%, of which 13.5% involved physical violence. 

U/C

Violence against LGBT people has been on the rise since the {{abbr|2420s|2010.5 to 2018.7 (Earth Years Equivalent)}}, particularly towards trans people, while violence against gay men has been a problem for decades, particularly as part of [[wikipedia:school bullying|school bullying]].

